# =============================================================================
# .env.example -- every secret and host path the operator supplies. Copy to
# harness/.env, fill it in, `chmod 600 harness/.env`, and never commit it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THESE LIVE ON THE HOST. NONE OF THEM ENTERS THE CONTAINER UNASKED.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# inspect_swe injects a literal dummy provider key into the CLI's environment,
# and the two provider API domains are blocked at the host firewall
# (DOCKER-USER rules installed by ops/provision-box.sh), so a real provider key
# could not be used inside the container even with egress open. Model traffic
# reaches the provider over the sandbox bridge -- a proxy inside the container
# speaking to the host over docker-exec stdio -- and the host is where every
# call is metered and priced. That is why the sandbox needs no provider
# credential, and why the only host values that ever cross into it are the
# ones you name in AGENT_ENV_KEYS (placeholders.txt; section 3 below).
#
# If you ever find yourself putting a key into container/Dockerfile,
# container/compose.yaml or the workspace seed, stop: the design has been
# broken somewhere upstream, and a ten-hour agent run is the wrong place to
# discover it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How this file is read
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inspect calls python-dotenv's find_dotenv(usecwd=True), which walks UP from
# the working directory, then load_dotenv() WITHOUT override -- a variable
# already exported in your shell wins over this file. ops/run.sh launches
# `inspect eval` from harness/, so this file is picked up automatically, and
# because Inspect loads it into its own process environment before it spawns
# `docker compose`, CRUX_IMAGE and CRUX_DATA_DIR below also reach compose's
# ${VAR} interpolation.
#
# Do not create harness/container/.env: `docker compose` interpolates a .env
# sitting beside the compose file into a service definition that runs
# untrusted code for hours. Keep secrets here, one level up.
# =============================================================================


# ── 1. Model provider ── the arm's key is REQUIRED ───────────────────────────
# Inspect's providers read exactly these names. Only the key for the arm you
# run (ARM in placeholders.txt) is needed; ops/run.sh checks for it.

# console.anthropic.com -> Settings -> API keys. ARM=claude.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# platform.openai.com -> API keys. ARM=codex.
OPENAI_API_KEY=


# ── 2. Run host configuration ── not secret ──────────────────────────────────

# The image tag ops/provision-box.sh builds from container/Dockerfile and
# container/compose.yaml runs. Use an explicit tag: an untagged 'latest' is how
# a rebuilt image quietly changes the toolchain under a run.
CRUX_IMAGE=crux-harness:REPLACE-WITH-BUILD-TAG

# [optional] The AWS CLI profile ops/provision-box.sh and ops/collect.sh use (a name from
# ~/.aws/config; not a secret). AWS_PROFILE in the environment wins over this line. Set it
# so every box lands in one account on a machine whose default profile is someone else's;
# an SSO profile needs `aws sso login --profile <name>` before the scripts run.
# AWS_PROFILE=crux

# [optional] Host directory of pre-staged data (see data/README.md), mounted
# read-only at /data in the container. Leave unset for no /data mount. Locally
# this is the directory ops/stage_data.sh hashed; ops/provision-box.sh copies it
# to the box and rewrites this line to the box path.
# CRUX_DATA_DIR=/absolute/path/to/harness/data

# [optional, on by default in ops/run.sh] Bounded transcripts: keep only the
# most recent events resident in memory instead of the whole run.
# INSPECT_TRANSCRIPT_BOUNDED=1


# ── 3. Keys the agent may use ── passed into the container only by name ──────
# The agent's brief (AGENTS.md § Environment) names three optional resources:
# GPU compute (RunPod), an experiment-LLM budget (OpenRouter), and an external
# review service (refine.ink). Each is a credential defined HERE and crosses
# into the container only if its name is listed in AGENT_ENV_KEYS in
# placeholders.txt -- loop/agents.py copies exactly those names out of the
# host environment (which Inspect loaded from this file) and records which
# names were passed, never the values. A name listed but not set here is
# skipped silently; the agent then finds the key absent, writes n/a in its
# ledger, and does not plan around that resource.
#
# Create dedicated keys with their own credit limits (OPENROUTER_BUDGET and
# CLOUD_SPEND_LIMIT in placeholders.txt are what the agent is told; the
# provider-side limit is what actually stops spend). ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and
# OPENAI_API_KEY can never be listed: the container is metered through the
# bridge, and configure.sh refuses them.

# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...     # the experiments' LLM calls
# RUNPOD_API_KEY=rpa_...              # GPU pods
# REFINE_INK_API_KEY=...              # external reviews (REQUIRE_EXTERNAL_REVIEWS=1 only if set)
